  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "14.13.yes",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  Let's write the Date class. Date stores information about the day, month, and year in private variables, in addition to a std::vector of the number of days in each month. Write accessor functions for each variable, keeping in mind the valid values each variable can take. In addition, write the default constructor, which initializes the date to January 1, 2000. Write another constructor which takes in a day, month, and year in that order.     class Date {    private:    int day, month, year;    std::vector<int> daysInMonth = { 31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31 };    public:    Date() { day = 1; month = 1; year = 2000; }    Date() { day = 0; month = 0; year = 0; }    Date(int d, int m, int y) { day = d; month = m; year = y; }    int getDay() { return day; }    int getMonth() { return month; }    int getYear() { return year; }    void setDay(int d) { if (d > 0 && d < 32) day = d; }    void setMonth(int m) { if (m > 0 && m < 13) month = m; }    void setYear(int y) { year = y; }    void setDay(int d) { if (d > 1 && d < 31) day = d; }    void setMonth(int m) { if (m >= 0 && m <= 12) month = m; }    void setYear(int y) { if (y >= 2000 && y < 3000) year = y; }    };    "
