  "body": "  We took a look at std::vector s in chapter 10, where we saw how we could add data to the end of a std::vector and remove data from the end of a std::vector . But what if we wanted to add and remove things at the beginning of a std::vector ? Or we wanted to print out a std::vector without painfully constructing a loop every time? We can create our own MyVector class! Write the MyVector class, which has a std::vector of int s as a private member variable. Also write the default constructor.   #include <iostream> #include <vector> \/\/ Write the class definition for MyVector here.   Below is the class definition of MyVector . We use the public and private keywords to separate public and private members of our class. The default constructor sets size to 0.   #include <iostream> #include <vector> class MyVector { private: std::vector<int> elements; public: MyVector() {}; };    "
